In this experiment, I learned how to connect two different AI models using a single Python application. The main purpose of this experiment was to create one common interface that supports both Gemini and Ollama. First, I created a common provider class and then implemented separate classes for Gemini and Ollama. Gemini is an online AI service that requires an API key, whereas Ollama allows us to run an AI model locally on our computer. I used the llama3.2 model through Ollama for the local execution. After creating the provider classes, I developed an LLMClient class that accepts the selected provider and sends the user's question to it. In the main.py file, I added an option to select either Gemini or Ollama. After selecting the model, I entered a question and the program generated an answer using the selected provider. I also created a test file named test_client.py to check whether the common client was working properly. For testing, I used a FakeProvider that returns a sample response without connecting to an actual AI service. I executed the program in VS Code after installing the required packages and setting up the selected provider. Through this experiment, I understood how different AI models can be connected using a common interface and how classes and objects help us organize Python programs.
